1285-### 2333 — An open directory handle proves a boundary on Windows and proves nothing on Unix: bind mutation to the object, not the pathname
1286-
1287-- ROOT CAUSE: `buildcache.Sweep` validated the cache root with `O_NOFOLLOW` handles held open for the whole pass, then retired entries with pathname `os.CreateTemp`/`os.Rename`/`os.RemoveAll`. An open fd does not pin later pathname resolution on Unix, so exchanging the cache-root path between validation and retirement redirected both the rename and the deletion into the replacement tree — outside the proven boundary the comment claimed to guarantee.
1288:- FIX: `sweepRoot` pairs the validated `protectedDir` with an `os.Root` opened on the same path and accepted only when `os.SameFile` says it is the same directory object; all mutation goes through `root.mutator.Rename`/`RemoveAll`, and `inspectUnexpected` asserts the candidate's parent is that same object (`internal/buildcache/collect.go`).
1289-- FINDING: `os.Root` (Go 1.24/1.25) is the right primitive — handle-relative, no-follow, escape-refusing rename and recursive removal on both platforms — but `os.OpenRoot` still resolves its own argument by pathname, so it needs an independent identity check against a separately validated handle to close the window.
1290-- FINDING: Windows closes it one component earlier. Handles opened without `FILE_SHARE_DELETE` make the cache root un-renamable while the sweep holds it; on the native run the adversarial swap was refused by the OS.
1291-- NOTE: negative control is what proved the test — restoring pathname `Rename`/`RemoveAll` makes the swap test fail with "the sweep acted inside the replacement tree".
1292-- SCOPE: `TASK-260720-1ljev5` rework cycle 1; `internal/buildcache/collect.go`, `protection_{unix,windows,unsupported}.go`.
